          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"bronte-tenant-of-wildfell-hall-e78441fff281b5dc\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Arthur’s “that I love you wholly and entirely” is principally an avowal presented as true, reinforced by his contrast between passing fancies and love that endures “steadily, and for ever.” The marital setting and enduring language weakly invoke relational constancy, but he does not clearly undertake a new obligation or promise. Its controlled, defensive delivery supplies no independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The claim of enduring marital love gives slight support to P, but it is more naturally an assertion used to reassure and excuse his conduct than a distinct undertaking.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Arthur directly speaks the love avowal to Helen, although the event is reproduced within Helen’s first-person diary narrative.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Arthur makes the avowal after Helen catches him behaving intimately with Annabella. He uses it to minimize the incident, reassure Helen, and distinguish supposedly transient attractions from his enduring love for her. His “lurking smile,” subsequent lack of contrition, and insistence that no harm would have occurred had Helen not seen him cast doubt on his sincerity and make the avowal strategically self-exculpatory; those features do not alter its core truth-conditional mode.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“that I love you wholly and entirely”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit first-person present-tense avowal strongly supports T.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“those fancies are here and gone like a flash of lightning, while my love for you burns on steadily, and for ever, like the sun”\",\"supports\":\"The contrast presents his love as a stable, enduring state and gives limited secondary support to relational commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"He pauses, approaches, kneels near Helen’s level, and begins “in a low voice,” later adding the qualification “with a lurking smile.”\",\"supports\":\"The deliberate, controlled delivery favors a strategic avowal rather than a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Arthur has just been seen kissing Annabella’s hand; afterward he minimizes the conduct and says that without Helen seeing it, “it would have done no harm.”\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding exchange frames the avowal as defensive reassurance or manipulation and raises doubts about sincerity without changing its T/P/E force.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the dominant avowal, with slight P support for invoked marital constancy. The strategic and potentially insincere context is adequately described contextually, and no important core force remains outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
